  "runner_up_case": "The strongest honest case for TRUE rests on circumstantial convergence plus the structural invisibility of German judgments. (1) Identity is confirmed: Hagenauer GmbH, AG Kempten HRB 4135, Mittagstr. 7, 87509 Immenstadt, a construction general contractor (upscale hotels, healthcare facilities, office buildings, cruise-ship outfitting) — an industry with an inherently high frequency of supplier disputes. (2) The company's trajectory fits the claim: Northdata shows it as 'Immenstadt (i. L.)' — in liquidation — and insolvenzindex.de records insolvency proceedings at AG Kempten dated 1.6.2026. Construction firms rarely slide from ordinary operation into insolvency without a preceding tail of unpaid supplier invoices, and suppliers of distressed contractors commonly obtain payment titles (Mahnbescheid, Versaeumnisurteil, Vollstreckungsbefehl) in the one-to-two years before the insolvency filing; 2024 is exactly that window. (3) Decisively, German first-instance civil judgments are not published in any public database: default judgments and payment orders in particular never appear online. The team's null results across Justizportal, Bundesanzeiger and six web searches are precisely what we would observe even if the claim were true, so they carry almost no exculpatory weight — this is not a US-style docket search where absence of hits means absence of cases. (4) Nothing in the record contradicts the claim: no source states the company had no 2024 litigation, and no namesake confusion has been demonstrated. On this record TRUE cannot be affirmed — there is zero direct evidence of a judgment — but UNVERIFIABLE here reflects the opacity of German court records rather than affirmative doubt about the claim, and the insolvency/liquidation evidence materially raises its prior probability. One targeted look at the company's Creditreform payment-remark record (Hartevermerke with dates) could still surface a 2024 court title from a supplier and justify upgrading to TRUE.",
